{
  "overall_diagnosis": "Failures stem from benchmark-aligned solution classification within Azure Virtual WAN BGP scenarios, not from detecting oscillation/route leak. The agent correctly identified oscillation and leak conditions but misclassified multiple Azure-native, policy-level mitigations (Virtual WAN routing intent, UDR/route-table overrides, and RPKI origin validation) as ineffective. This indicates the azure-bgp skill under-specifies how to map common Azure mitigation proposals to the evaluator’s two outcomes (oscillation_resolved, route_leak_resolved), and needs clearer decision rules and examples distinguishing valid policy fixes from prohibited connectivity-removal actions.",
  "diagnosed_skills": [
    {
      "path": "azure-bgp/SKILL.md",
      "confidence": 0.85,
      "diagnosis": "The skill covers oscillation/leak detection and warns against prohibited fixes, but lacks explicit, benchmark-aligned guidance for evaluating remediation proposals. In particular, it does not clearly state that Virtual WAN routing intent and UDR/route-table next-hop overrides can be considered valid cycle breakers (oscillation_resolved) and can also prevent hub-to-hub transit/leaks (route_leak_resolved) in the simplified benchmark model. It also under-specifies how to treat security validation like RPKI origin validation, which the benchmark may credit as leak-mitigating even if it’s not classic valley-free enforcement. This ambiguity leads to systematic misclassification of valid mitigations as False/False.",
      "repair_instruction": "Add a dedicated “Solution Classification (Benchmark-Aligned)” section that maps common Azure Virtual WAN mitigation phrases to expected boolean outcomes. Include:\n1) Decision rules:\n- Set oscillation_resolved=True when the action breaks a preference cycle or enforces deterministic next-hop/path selection (e.g., routing intent that constrains inter-hub routing, UDR/route-table override forcing a stable next hop, export/import policy that removes one competing path, communities that change availability).\n- Set route_leak_resolved=True when the action stops hub-to-hub propagation/acceptance of provider-learned routes (e.g., export policy blocking provider routes to peer hub, ingress filtering rejecting routes with specific ASNs/AS_PATH patterns, no-export/no-advertise communities, routing intent enforcing centralized routing constraints).\n- Treat RPKI origin validation as route_leak_resolved=True when it would reject illegitimate/leaked prefixes per the benchmark’s simplified semantics; typically oscillation_resolved remains False unless it also changes path preference.\n2) Worked examples/matrix: Provide example solution strings for Virtual WAN routing intent, UDR override, export/ingress filtering, no-export communities, and RPKI with expected (oscillation_resolved, route_leak_resolved).\n3) Explicit non-fixes list for this benchmark: timers, dampening, ECMP tweaks, restarts, max-prefix, generic ‘tune BGP’ suggestions that don’t alter policy/propagation should default to False/False.\n4) Reinforce invariants: do not propose/credit disabling BGP sessions, shutting down peerings, or removing connectivity; these remain invalid even if they would stop symptoms."
    }
  ],
  "unselected_skills": []
}
